# Enterprise Features

- EE Files are any files with `.ee.` in the filename, any path under an `ee/` directory, or any package/directory ending in `.ee`. Keep the legal boundary file-level: do not mix core and EE behavior in the same source file.
- Core code may be imported by EE code, but core code must not import EE files. If a core route needs optional EE behavior, split the implementation so the core path remains usable without paywalled code.
- Every EE entry point that performs paywalled behavior must call `assertEnterpriseLicense(<featureId>)` before doing work. Current feature IDs include `sso`, `multi-tenant`, `ai`, and reserved `ai-premium`.
- License checks must fail closed. Do not silently degrade, bypass, or substitute a core approximation for an unlicensed Enterprise feature.
- Local development can use `TRUSTALO_LICENSE_DEV_BYPASS=1`; never rely on it in production paths. Production must require `TRUSTALO_LICENSE_KEY` validated by `packages/license`.
- When adding a new EE feature, update `packages/license/src/types.ts`, `docs/enterprise.md`, the README license/EE section, and any relevant feature docs.
- Tests for EE behavior should cover both entitled and unentitled paths. EE-only tests may set `TRUSTALO_LICENSE_DEV_BYPASS=1` explicitly.
- Do not remove, weaken, or hide license checks. Changing `packages/license` or the trusted public-key path requires focused tests and clear review notes.
